what is domain adaptation  when fine-tuning a pre-trained model on  a new data set the fine-tuned model we  obtain will make predictions that are  attuned to this new data set  when the two models are trained with the  same task  we can then compare their predictions on  the same input  the predictions of the two models will  be different in a way that reflects the  differences between the two data sets  a phenomenon we call domain adaptation  let's look at an example with mass  language modeling  by comparing the outputs of the  pre-trained distilbert model with the  version fine-tuned in chapter 7 of the  course link below  the retrain model makes generic  predictions whereas the fine-tuned model  has its first two predictions linked to  cinema  since it was fine-tuned on a movie  reviews data set it's perfectly normal  to see it adapted in suggestions like  this  notice how it keeps the same prediction  as a pretrained model afterward  even if the function model adapts to the  new data set it's not forgetting what it  was pre-trained on  this is another example on a translation  task  on top with the pre-trained french  english model and at the bottom the  version we find unique chapter 7.  the top model is retrained on lots of  texts and leaves technical english terms  like plugging in an email and changes  the translation  both are perfectly understood by french  people  the data set picked for the fine tuning  is a set of technical texts where  special attention was picked on  translating everything in french  as a result the fine-tuned model picked  that a bit and translated both plugin  and email
 

